kant-groundwork-4.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1 has "sets_end → end_matter"; kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1.3 have "end_matter"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
kant-groundwork-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "Rational nature sets before itself an end, which distinguishes it from the rest of nature" and "This end would be the matter of every good will" are false. Is S2, "This end would be the matter of every good will.", true then?
kant-groundwork-4.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1 and kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1.3 have "subject_good_will" and "no_postpone"; kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1.2 has "no_postpone" and "no_postpone → subject_good_will" and "subject_good_will → subject_end"; they differ when only "The subject of all possible ends is also the subject of a possible absolutely good will" and "Such a will cannot without contradiction be postponed to any other object" are true, which the first allows
kant-groundwork-4.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "The subject of all possible ends is also the subject of a possible absolutely good will" and "Such a will cannot without contradiction be postponed to any other object" are true and "This end is nothing but the subject of all possible ends" is false. Is S5, "Now this end can be nothing but the subject of all possible ends, since this is also the subject of a possible absolutely good will; for such a will cannot without contradiction be postponed to any other object.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1 has "end_matter → effected ∨ independent" and "independent → negative" and "negative ∧ subject_good_will ∧ no_postpone → subject_end" and "negative ∧ subject_end ∧ limit_eq → equiv"; kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1.2 has "¬effected → independent" and "independent → negative" and "negative ∧ subject_end ∧ limit_eq → equiv"; they differ when only "The end is conceived only negatively: as what we must never act against, never to be regarded merely as means, but in every volition esteemed as an end likewise", "The subject of all possible ends is also the subject of a possible absolutely good will", "Such a will cannot without contradiction be postponed to any other object", "This end is nothing but the subject of all possible ends", "Limiting my maxim, in using means for every end, by the condition of its holding good as a law for every subject comes to the same thing as the fundamental principle that the subject of all ends, the rational being himself, never be employed merely as means but in every case as an end likewise" and "The principle "so act in regard to every rational being that he may always have place in thy maxim as an end in himself" is essentially identical with "act upon a maxim which involves its own universal validity for every rational being"" are true, which the first allows; kant-groundwork-4.logic.r1.3 has "¬effected → independent" and "independent → negative" and "subject_good_will ∧ no_postpone → subject_end" and "limit_eq ∧ subject_end ∧ negative → equiv"
